SECTION 2 - TRAIN/TEST SPLIT AND LEAKAGE-SAFE PREPROCESSING
==============================================================================
Training set size : 19,460 rows (80%)
Test set size     : 4,866 rows (20%)
Split settings    : test_size=0.2, random_state=42, shuffle=True
Accepted share    : 0.4416 in train, 0.4303 in test

Training-set statistics (computed from the 80% training split only):
feature                       median        mean         std
gpa                           3.8500      3.8134      0.3085
gre                         316.0000    311.1799     28.8447
gre_v                       161.0000    160.9873      1.9706
gre_aw                        4.5000      4.7809      5.2542
ms_vs_phd                     1.0000      0.7219      0.4481
international_vs_local        0.0000      0.4706      0.4991

Why these statistics come from the training set only:
  The test set stands in for applicants the model has never seen. If
  the medians, means, and standard deviations were computed over the
  full dataset, every test row would have shaped the numbers used to
  fill and scale the training rows - that is data leakage, and the
  reported test score would be optimistic because test information
  reached the model during training. Fitting on the training split
  alone keeps the test set a genuine estimate of future performance.

==============================================================================
SECTION 3 - TWO-LAYER NEURAL NETWORK (NUMPY ONLY)
==============================================================================
Architecture      : 6 inputs -> 6 hidden units -> 1 output unit
Activations       : sigmoid after the hidden layer, sigmoid after the output layer
Loss function     : mean squared error
Initialization    : weights ~ N(0.0, 0.1), biases = 0
Hyperparameters   : RANDOM_SEED=42, HIDDEN_UNITS=6, LEARNING_RATE=0.05,
                    MAX_EPOCHS=10000, PATIENCE=100

Parameter shapes:
  w1 (6, 6)   one weight per (input feature, hidden unit)
  b1 (1, 6)   one bias per hidden unit
  w2 (6, 1)   one weight per (hidden unit, output unit)
  b2 (1, 1)   the output unit's bias

The hidden layer computes a1 = sigmoid(x @ w1 + b1): six different
non-linear blends of the six standardized inputs. The output layer
computes a2 = sigmoid(a1 @ w2 + b2), one weighted summary of those
blends squashed into (0, 1). Bounded, rising with the evidence for
acceptance, and trained against 0/1 targets, a2 reads as a
probability-like score - but MSE training leaves it uncalibrated, so
it is not a literal admission probability.

