SECTION 7 - PREDICTIONS FOR ARTIFICIAL APPLICANTS
==============================================================================
                    profile    gpa      gre    gre_v  gre_aw  ms_vs_phd  international_vs_local  probability  label   status
  Strong PhD, international 3.9500 335.0000 165.0000  5.0000     1.0000                  1.0000       0.3272      0 Rejected
          Strong PhD, local 3.9500 335.0000 165.0000  5.0000     1.0000                  0.0000       0.3469      0 Rejected
     Average Masters, local 3.4000 305.0000 152.0000  3.5000     0.0000                  0.0000       0.7287      1 Accepted
Weak Masters, international 2.9000 295.0000 145.0000  3.0000     0.0000                  1.0000       0.6820      1 Accepted
Strong GPA, no GRE reported 3.9000      NaN      NaN     NaN     1.0000                  0.0000       0.3294      0 Rejected

What these predictions show:
  Scores span 0.3272 (Strong PhD, international) to 0.7287 (Average Masters, local),
  and 2 of 5 profiles clear the 0.5 threshold.
  The ordering is driven by degree type, not by credentials: every
  Masters profile outscores every PhD profile, even though the top
  PhD applicant carries a full GPA point and 40 GRE points more than
  the weakest Masters one. That is the data speaking - 75.9% of
  Masters rows here were accepted versus 31.6% of PhD rows - but
  it means the network is largely reproducing base rates by degree
  rather than judging an applicant's strength.
  Within a degree the numbers do move the score the expected way:
  the average Masters applicant scores +0.0467 against the
  weak one, and citizenship shifts two otherwise identical PhD
  applicants by -0.0197. Both effects are small next to
  the degree flag.
  The 'no GRE reported' applicant is scored as if holding median GRE
  values - exactly what median-filling does. About nine in ten real
  rows lack GRE scores, so most predictions lean on GPA, degree, and
  citizenship whether or not test scores were supplied.
